// ============================================================
// BIOGRAPHIES — narrative notes, keyed by leader id
// ============================================================
// These are the longer biographical/analytical paragraphs shown
// under "Biography" in each profile. They are stored separately
// from data/leaders.js so the index stays lean and bios are easy
// to grow. Precedence in the app:
//   a leader's own `bio` field (your edits)  >  BIOS[id]  >  none
// Add a bio for any leader by adding "<id>": "…text…" below.
// ============================================================

window.BIOS = {

  // ---------------- earlier presidents ----------------
  washington: "Virginia planter, surveyor, and reluctant revolutionary who became the indispensable man of the American founding twice over — first as the general who kept a starving Continental Army together for eight years, then as the president who defined an office that had no precedent. His genuine reluctance was his greatest asset: he could be trusted with power precisely because he kept demonstrating he would give it up, resigning his commission in 1783 (astonishing a watching world) and stepping down after two terms in 1797. Nearly everything he did — the cabinet, the inaugural address, the farewell warning against faction and foreign entanglement — became a template, because he knew every act would be copied. The tragedy inseparable from the greatness: the man who embodied a republic of liberty enslaved more than three hundred people at Mount Vernon.",

  jefferson: "Author of the Declaration of Independence, architect, scientist, diplomat, and the most eloquent contradiction in American history — a philosopher of human equality who owned and never freed the enslaved people whose labor funded Monticello. As president he governed more pragmatically than he theorized: the small-government apostle doubled the nation's size with the Louisiana Purchase on constitutional authority he privately doubted he had. He built the first true party organization and preferred to lead through dinners, letters, and personal persuasion rather than command. His mind ranged across everything from the University of Virginia to the plow, and his rivalry-and-friendship with Adams framed the young republic's argument with itself.",

  jackson: "Orphaned by the Revolution, self-made frontier lawyer, duelist, and the hero of New Orleans, Jackson rode a wave of expanded white male suffrage into a presidency he cast as the direct instrument of 'the people' against entrenched elites. He shattered the old order — killing the Second Bank of the United States, inventing the spoils system, and bending the office toward mass democracy and personal will. That same will produced its darkest chapter: the Indian Removal Act and the Trail of Tears, forced displacement he pursued in defiance of the Supreme Court. Ferociously loyal and ferociously vindictive, he remains the template for the populist strongman inside a democratic frame.",

  lincoln: "A largely self-taught prairie lawyer who lost more elections than he won before 1860, Lincoln entered the presidency as the country broke apart and proved the greatest political mind ever to hold the office. He managed a war, a fractious cabinet of men who thought themselves his betters, and a shifting coalition of abolitionists and border-state pragmatists — moving on emancipation exactly when the moment could bear it, never before. His weapon was language: the Gettysburg Address and Second Inaugural redefined what the war and the nation were for. Murdered days after Appomattox, he became the secular saint of American union, his patience and moral timing the enduring case study in democratic leadership.",

  juarez: "Born to Zapotec peasants in Oaxaca and orphaned young, Juárez learned Spanish as a teenager, trained as a lawyer, and rose to embody Mexican liberalism and the rule of law. As president he drove La Reforma — separating church and state, subordinating the army, and asserting constitutional government — then carried the republic through the French intervention and Maximilian's puppet empire, governing from a black carriage that never stopped moving. His austere persistence made the constitution itself the true sovereign; his victory preserved Mexican independence and became a founding myth of the modern nation. 'Respect for the rights of others is peace' remains his signature line.",

  // ---------------- 20th-century U.S. presidents ----------------
  mckinley: "The last Civil War veteran to reach the White House and, in many tellings, the first modern president. A genial Ohio congressman and governor carried to power by Mark Hanna's pioneering, money-driven campaign machine, McKinley presided over the Spanish-American War, the annexation of the Philippines, Hawaii, and Puerto Rico, and America's decisive turn from continental republic to overseas power. He governed through quiet consensus and a professionalizing executive rather than bombast, expanding presidential reach abroad while keeping a calm Midwestern surface. His assassination by an anarchist in 1901 handed the office — and the century — to his restless vice president, Theodore Roosevelt.",

  troosevelt: "Sickly asthmatic boy turned cowboy, war hero, trust-buster, and force of nature, TR became at 42 the youngest president ever and the first to treat the office as a platform of pure energy — the 'bully pulpit.' He busted monopolies, built the Panama Canal, seized 230 million acres for conservation, brokered the Russo-Japanese peace (winning a Nobel), and expanded the presidency's reach on the theory that the president may do anything the Constitution doesn't forbid. Hyperactive, self-dramatizing, and morally certain, he redefined the modern activist presidency. His later third-party 'Bull Moose' run split the Republicans and handed 1912 to Wilson.",

  taft: "The only man to lead both the executive and judicial branches, Taft wanted the Supreme Court his whole life and endured the presidency as a detour. A superb administrator with a judicial temperament, he actually pursued more antitrust suits than Roosevelt — but by statute and courtroom rather than showmanship, and he had no gift for the political theater the office had come to demand. His conservative drift and personal fallout with TR fractured the Republican Party and cost him 1912 in a humiliating third-place finish. He got his wish in 1921, appointed Chief Justice, and was, by his own account, far happier.",

  wilson: "A political-science professor and president of Princeton who leapt to the New Jersey governorship and the White House within three years, Wilson governed by moral principle and soaring rhetoric. His first term produced landmark reforms — the Federal Reserve, the income tax, the FTC, antitrust law; his second was consumed by the World War, the Fourteen Points, and his crusade for a League of Nations to end war itself. That crusade broke on his own rigidity: he refused to compromise with the Senate, suffered a devastating stroke while barnstorming for the treaty, and spent his final months an invalid whose wife screened the presidency. Idealist and racial reactionary both, he re-segregated the federal government even as he preached self-determination abroad.",

  harding: "A handsome, affable Ohio newspaperman and senator who looked the part and promised a weary nation a 'return to normalcy' after war and Wilsonian crusade. Genuinely warm and genuinely out of his depth, Harding assembled a cabinet mixing real talent (Hughes, Hoover, Mellon) with the cronies of the 'Ohio Gang' who looted the government behind his back. He died suddenly in 1923 — popular and mourned — just before Teapot Dome and other scandals surfaced to sink his reputation to the bottom of most presidential rankings. His fall is the enduring lesson in the cost of over-trusting friends and under-watching the space you leave them.",

  coolidge: "'Silent Cal' — a taciturn Vermont-bred lawyer who rose through Massachusetts politics, broke the 1919 Boston police strike, and inherited the presidency on Harding's death, then restored its dignity by doing and saying as little as possible. He believed on principle that the chief business of the American people was business, that most problems would solve themselves if a president resisted the urge to meddle, and that words unspoken caused no trouble. His laissez-faire calm suited the booming twenties and made him quietly popular; critics later charged that the same passivity let the imbalances behind 1929 build unchecked. He declined to run again with characteristic economy: 'I do not choose to run.'",

  hoover: "Orphaned Iowa farm boy turned self-made mining millionaire and the 'Great Humanitarian' who fed millions in war-torn Europe — Hoover reached the White House as perhaps the most globally admired administrator of his age, and the Depression destroyed him. His deep faith in voluntarism, business-led cooperation, and rugged individualism left him temperamentally unable to deploy federal power at the scale the collapse demanded, and his managerial, data-driven style read as cold as breadlines lengthened and 'Hoovervilles' spread. Brilliant and decent, he became the symbol of government paralysis before catastrophe, buried in a 1932 landslide. His long, productive post-presidency partly rehabilitated the man if not the presidency.",

  truman: "A Missouri farmer, failed haberdasher, and machine-politics senator who became vice president almost by accident and inherited the presidency in April 1945 knowing nothing of the atomic bomb — then made some of the century's heaviest decisions. Plain-spoken and decisive ('The buck stops here'), he ordered the bombs dropped, recognized Israel, launched the Marshall Plan and NATO, integrated the armed forces, and built the entire Cold War national-security apparatus. He asserted civilian supremacy by firing the insubordinate war hero MacArthur, at ruinous cost to his own popularity. Dismissed as a failure when he left, 'Give 'em Hell Harry' has risen steadily in historians' estimation as the model of the gutsy, accountable executive.",

  eisenhower: "Supreme Allied Commander in Europe, the general who organized D-Day and held a fractious coalition of prima-donna allies together, Eisenhower brought a lifetime of command to a presidency he ran with deceptive ease. Behind the genial, golfing, grandfatherly image was a shrewd operator practicing what scholars later called 'hidden-hand' leadership — steering events while letting subordinates take the visible credit and blame. He ended the Korean War, built the Interstate Highway System, restrained the military budget, and warned in farewell of the 'military-industrial complex' he knew from the inside. Deliberate, systematic, and allergic to recklessness, he gave the anxious 1950s a steadiness that looked like inaction and was in fact tight control.",

  jfk: "Wartime PT-boat hero, Pulitzer author, and son of a ruthless political dynasty, Kennedy brought youth, glamour, and cool rationalism to the White House — and a steep on-the-job learning curve. The Bay of Pigs fiasco in 1961 taught him to distrust unanimous expert advice; a year later he redesigned his own decision process and used it to walk the world back from nuclear war in the Cuban Missile Crisis. He launched the moon program, confronted (belatedly) the civil-rights revolution, and made his brother Bobby an unprecedented deputy. Assassinated in Dallas in November 1963, he left more myth than record — but the missile-crisis case study in engineering better group decisions is real and enduring.",

  lbj: "The most effective legislative operator ever to reach the presidency — a towering, overbearing Texan who read every senator's fears, debts, and ambitions and applied 'the Treatment' at point-blank range. Elevated by Kennedy's murder, he drove through the Civil Rights Act, Voting Rights Act, Medicare, Medicaid, and the whole Great Society by knowing the machinery of Congress better than anyone alive. That same mastery failed him in Vietnam, where he micromanaged bombing targets at Tuesday lunches and was slowly consumed by a war he neither wanted nor understood. He declined to seek reelection in 1968, a broken giant — the fullest illustration of how domestic genius and foreign catastrophe can inhabit one presidency.",

  nixon: "A grocer's son from California whose resentment of elites fueled a relentless climb — congressman, red-hunting senator, Eisenhower's vice president, humiliated loser in 1960 and 1962, and then the ultimate comeback in 1968. In office he was a paradox: visionary abroad, opening China, pursuing détente with Moscow, and remaking the geopolitical board, while nursing a siege mentality that built an enemies list and a covert-operations culture inside his own White House. Watergate — the break-in and, fatally, the coverup he directed — grew straight out of that insularity, and in 1974 he became the only president to resign. Few careers so completely fuse strategic brilliance and self-destruction.",

  ford: "The only person to serve as president without being elected to national office, Ford was a steady, decent Michigan congressman and House minority leader tapped to replace the disgraced Agnew, then thrust into the top job when Nixon fell. His assignment was restoration — 'our long national nightmare is over' — and he governed with an open, collegial cabinet as deliberate contrast to Nixon's bunker. His controversial pardon of Nixon, taken alone on the judgment that the country needed to move on, likely cost him the 1976 election but looks wiser in hindsight. Amid stagflation and the fall of Saigon, his plainness was the point.",

  carter: "A Naval Academy nuclear engineer, peanut farmer, and one-term Georgia governor who ran as a scandal-weary nation's honest outsider and won — then found that outsider status left him few allies even in his own party. His mastery of detail was legendary and sometimes self-defeating (he famously waded into the smallest scheduling decisions), crowding out the strategic delegation the office needs. He brokered the Camp David Accords and championed human rights as foreign-policy doctrine, but stagflation, the energy crisis, and the 444-day Iran hostage ordeal sank him in 1980. His four-decade post-presidency of diplomacy, disease eradication, and house-building became arguably the most admired in American history.",

  ghwbush: "A war-hero pilot, oilman, congressman, UN ambassador, envoy to China, CIA director, and vice president, Bush reached the White House with perhaps the deepest résumé of any modern president — and governed as a prudent, patrician internationalist who conducted diplomacy by personal phone call. Backed by a seasoned, collegial team (Baker, Scowcroft, Powell, Cheney), he steered the peaceful end of the Cold War and assembled the broad coalition that reversed Iraq's invasion of Kuwait, all with a deliberate restraint that declined to gloat as the Berlin Wall fell. Breaking his 'read my lips' tax pledge and a sour economy cost him reelection in 1992. History has been kinder to his careful stewardship of a hinge moment than voters were.",

  clinton: "A brilliant, undisciplined policy omnivore from small-town Arkansas, Clinton rose on prodigious talent and appetite to become the first Baby Boomer president and the defining 'New Democrat' of the post-Cold-War boom. He governed by triangulation and permanent campaign — balancing between parties, declaring 'the era of big government is over,' presiding over surpluses and welfare reform after his health-care overhaul collapsed. His freewheeling, late-night policy seminars showcased a formidable mind and a chronic indiscipline that produced the Lewinsky scandal and his 1998 impeachment (he was acquitted and left office popular). Empathetic 'I feel your pain' connection was his gift; self-control was his missing one.",

  fdr: "A patrician only child, stricken by polio at 39 and never to walk unaided again, Franklin Roosevelt turned private suffering into public empathy and won the presidency four times — the only person ever to do so. Facing the Depression and then a world war, he ran a deliberately chaotic administration, spawning overlapping 'alphabet agencies' and rival aides so that all information and every final choice flowed to him, behind a mask of serene public confidence. His New Deal remade the relationship between Americans and their government, his fireside chats redefined presidential communication, and his wartime leadership held together the alliance that beat fascism. He died in April 1945 as victory neared, leaving both a transformed nation and, in the imperial modern presidency, a lasting institutional legacy.",

  reagan: "A Depression-era lifeguard, radio announcer, and Hollywood actor who became a corporate spokesman, converted from New Deal Democrat to conservative standard-bearer, and reached the presidency at 69 as its oldest occupant to that point. He governed by vision and radical delegation — 'surround yourself with the best people, delegate authority, and don't interfere' — supplying the narrative, the nerve, and a handful of big calls while a troika ran operations. Tax cuts, deregulation, a military buildup, and a pivot from confronting to negotiating with Gorbachev framed his presidency and, in his telling, helped end the Cold War. The same hands-off style had a shadow side: the Iran-Contra scandal flourished in the spaces he left unwatched. The 'Great Communicator' reshaped American politics for a generation.",

  // ---------------- other 20th-century leaders ----------------
  lenin: "A provincial lawyer radicalized by his brother's execution, Lenin spent decades in exile theorizing and organizing the disciplined vanguard party that would seize a continent. His 'What Is To Be Done?' and the doctrine of democratic centralism — total debate until decision, total obedience after — became the organizational template for a century of revolutionary parties. Returned to Russia in a sealed train in 1917, he drove the Bolshevik coup, pulled Russia out of the war, and won a brutal civil war, improvising the one-party state and the secret police that outlived him. Incapacitated by strokes, his final 'Testament' warned against Stalin and went unheeded — a fatal failure of the one problem revolutions rarely solve: succession.",

  ataturk: "An Ottoman army officer who made his name defending Gallipoli, Mustafa Kemal rose from the wreckage of empire to found and remake a nation in his own image within fifteen years. As first president he abolished the caliphate, replaced Arabic script with a Latin alphabet, changed dress, law, calendar, and the very idea of Turkish identity — secular, Western-facing, national — largely by decree and force of will. He ruled through a single party as a modernization machine, with the army as guardian of his revolution, and earned the surname 'Atatürk,' father of the Turks. His authoritarian reforms remade a society; whether they took root or were imposed from above is Turkey's argument to this day.",

  stalin: "A Georgian cobbler's son, seminary dropout, and bank-robbing revolutionary who won supreme power not through oratory but by patiently controlling the party's personnel files as its 'grey blur' General Secretary. He built socialism in one country through forced collectivization and industrialization that killed millions by famine, then consumed his own party and army in the Great Terror, ruling through overlapping secret-police hierarchies and manufactured insecurity. He led the USSR to staggering losses and ultimate victory over Nazi Germany, emerging with an empire in Eastern Europe and a superpower rivalry. The archetype of bureaucratic totalitarian control, he showed that in a system built on fear, the machinery of terror eventually devours its own operators.",

  hitler: "A failed Austrian art student and WWI corporal who turned a fringe party and his own oratorical genius into absolute power over Germany, exploiting economic ruin, resentment of Versailles, and the machinery of a democracy he despised. His charismatic authority rested entirely on the Führer myth and a deliberately chaotic state of overlapping fiefdoms competing to 'work towards' his radicalizing will. That structure — power exercised without written orders, subordinates racing to anticipate his wishes — drove the Holocaust and a war of annihilation that killed tens of millions. He is the century's ultimate case study in charismatic authority as pure destruction, ending in a bunker as the country he had promised glory lay in ruins.",

  churchill: "Aristocrat, cavalry officer, war correspondent, and serial political apostate whose career looked finished in the 1930s — until his lonely warnings about Hitler made him the indispensable man of 1940. Fusing the premiership with the Ministry of Defence, he mobilized the English language itself as a weapon, making national defiance policy when the rational case was surrender. He bombarded generals with 'Action This Day' minutes and meddled ceaselessly, but knew when to take 'no' from men like Alan Brooke. Voted out even before the war ended, he returned as prime minister in the 1950s — a romantic, flawed, occasionally reactionary giant whose finest hour was also his nation's.",

  degaulle: "A maverick tank theorist and brigadier who, in June 1940, fled to London and declared himself the embodiment of Free France when France itself had surrendered — an act of almost absurd self-assurance that turned out to be right. Aloof, grandiose, and deliberately mysterious ('a certain idea of France'), he cultivated distance as a leadership method, laid out in his book 'The Edge of the Sword.' Returning to power in 1958's crisis, he wrote the Fifth Republic's presidential constitution around his own conception of authority and bound himself to the nation by referendum. He set direction and left execution to his premiers, and twice resigned rather than govern without a genuine mandate — the last time, in 1969, for good.",

  mao: "A peasant's son and library assistant who became the theorist of peasant revolution, Mao led the Communists through the Long March, the war with Japan, and victory in 1949, founding the People's Republic. He governed by permanent revolution and mass campaign, and repeatedly turned chaos against his own party whenever it threatened to constrain him — the Great Leap Forward's utopian industrialization triggered history's deadliest famine, and the Cultural Revolution convulsed the nation to reclaim power he felt slipping. He would retreat to the 'second line,' then destroy the deputies (Liu Shaoqi, Lin Biao) who accumulated standing in his absence. Founder and wrecker in one, he left a unified great power and tens of millions dead.",

  hochiminh: "A cook, gardener, and itinerant radical who spent three decades abroad — Paris, Moscow, southern China — building the networks and skills to free Vietnam from colonial rule, adopting many names before settling on Ho Chi Minh, 'he who enlightens.' He declared Vietnamese independence in 1945 quoting Jefferson, then led the long wars against France and the United States as the movement's unifying face and diplomat. Cultivating an ascetic 'Uncle Ho' image — sandals, simplicity, no family — he became a symbol while a collective politburo, increasingly led by Le Duan, made the hard operational calls. He died in 1969, six years before the reunification he embodied but did not live to see.",

  bengurion: "A Polish-born pioneer who emigrated to Ottoman Palestine, drained swamps, organized labor, and rose to lead the Zionist movement toward statehood, Ben-Gurion declared Israel's independence in 1948 against the advice of those who feared the coming war. His founding act of leadership was insisting the new state hold a monopoly on force — dismantling the Palmach and shelling the Irgun's Altalena to fold every militia into one army under civilian command (mamlachtiyut, 'statism'). Domineering on security and defense, he delegated more freely in economics, and twice retired to a desert kibbutz as a deliberate example. Blunt, visionary, and stubborn, he was the state's architect and its conscience-in-chief.",

  nasser: "An army officer who helped overthrow Egypt's monarchy in 1952 and emerged as the charismatic voice of Arab nationalism across an entire region via the radio waves of 'Voice of the Arabs.' Nationalizing the Suez Canal in 1956 and surviving the Anglo-French-Israeli assault turned a military setback into a political colossus, making him a transnational hero far beyond Egypt's borders. He built an officer-led one-party state and pursued pan-Arab union, but his charisma masked institutional rot: entrusting the army to his crony Amer produced the catastrophic 1967 defeat. He offered to resign; mass crowds demanded he stay, then he died in 1970 — the towering, tragic embodiment of Arab hopes and their limits.",

  nkrumah: "The London- and Pennsylvania-educated organizer who led the Gold Coast to become Ghana in 1957 — the first sub-Saharan colony to win independence — and made himself the prophet of pan-Africanism ('seek ye first the political kingdom'). He built a mass party and ambitious modernization projects, but his vision outran the economy, and the movement hardened into a one-party state and a personality cult with himself as 'Osagyefo,' the redeemer. Increasingly isolated at the center, he was overthrown by a military coup in 1966 while abroad in China. His arc — liberating charisma curdling into authoritarian isolation — became the archetypal story of the post-colonial founding father.",

  leekuanyew: "A Cambridge-trained lawyer who took a small, poor, ethnically divided port with no natural resources and, over three decades, forged one of the wealthiest states on earth — the defining case for clean technocratic authoritarianism. 'Poetry is a luxury we cannot afford,' he said, governing by survival economics, a meritocratic mandarinate paid private-sector wages, and a ruling party that managed dissent through the courts. Pragmatic to the core, he cared less for ideology than for what worked, and — unusually among strongmen — systematized his own succession, grooming and testing a second generation for years before actually handing over. Admired and criticized in equal measure, he made Singapore the model that other autocrats cite and other democracies uneasily study.",

  tito: "A Croatian-born metalworker and Comintern agent who led the Partisans' extraordinary resistance in occupied Yugoslavia, then ruled the federation he had welded from six quarrelsome republics for thirty-five years. His defining act was defiance: he broke with Stalin in 1948 and survived, then co-founded the Non-Aligned Movement, playing East against West to extract aid and autonomy from both. At home he balanced the nationalities against one another with himself as the indispensable arbiter, branding his system 'self-management socialism' as a third way. The glue was personal: when he died in 1980, the federation he embodied slowly came apart, and within a decade the country did too.",

  goldameir: "A Kyiv-born, Milwaukee-raised schoolteacher who emigrated to a kibbutz and rose through Labor Zionism to become Israel's first (and so far only) woman prime minister — the 'iron lady' before the phrase was Thatcher's. Blunt, grandmotherly, and formidable, she made decisions in an inner circle of trusted ministers that met, sometimes literally, in her kitchen. That cohesive 'kitchen cabinet' was also her weakness: deference to the defense establishment's complacent 'conception' left Israel surprised by the 1973 Yom Kippur attack, a trauma that ended her career. Her resignation the next year underscored how a tight circle of insiders can trade dissent for the comfort of consensus.",

  indira: "The only child of Nehru, dismissed early as a 'dumb doll' by party bosses who thought they could control her, Indira Gandhi instead became modern India's most dominant and divisive leader — 'India is Indira,' her supporters said. She centralized power personally inside a democracy, hollowing out her own Congress party into a vehicle of loyalty, and in 1975 suspended democracy itself, ruling by Emergency decree for nearly two years. She won a war that created Bangladesh and made India a nuclear power, but governed through a kitchen cabinet of aides and her sons as institutions decayed around her. Her assassination by her own bodyguards in 1984, after ordering troops into the Golden Temple, was the violent close of a personalist arc.",

  deng: "Twice purged under Mao and twice restored, the diminutive, chain-smoking survivor Deng Xiaoping emerged after 1978 as the paramount leader who never held the top title — and remade a fifth of humanity's economy. 'It doesn't matter whether the cat is black or white, so long as it catches mice': he opened Special Economic Zones as reversible experiments, 'crossing the river by feeling the stones,' and lifted hundreds of millions out of poverty. He restored collective leadership and term norms, and genuinely delegated to reformers — until they crossed political lines, as Hu Yaobang and Zhao Ziyang did. Tiananmen in 1989 marked his hard limit: economic liberty, yes; political challenge to the Party, never.",

  thatcher: "A grocer's daughter and Oxford-trained chemist who battered her way to the top of a class-bound party and became Britain's first woman prime minister and its most consequential of the century. Governing by conviction — 'the lady's not for turning,' consensus dismissed as 'the absence of leadership' — she broke the postwar settlement: privatization, deregulation, the defeat of the miners' strike, victory in the Falklands. She dominated her cabinet on substance through sheer stamina and preparation on four hours' sleep, purging the 'wets' who wavered. The same overbearing certainty that won three elections finally provoked the cabinet revolt that toppled her in 1990; 'Thatcherism' outlived her tenure by decades.",

  gorbachev: "A reform-minded farm boy who climbed the Soviet apparatus and, on reaching the top in 1985, tried to save the system by opening it — and lost control of the opening. Glasnost (openness) and perestroika (restructuring) unleashed forces no center could ride, as suppressed nationalisms, truths, and appetites surged into the space he created. Crucially, he refused the Tiananmen option: when Eastern Europe rose in 1989 he let it go, and the empire dissolved almost without bloodshed — a world-historical choice of restraint. Adored abroad and blamed at home for the loss of superpower and stability, he was the reformer undone by the reform, presiding over the peaceful end of the Cold War and his own country.",

  mandela: "A Xhosa chief's son, boxer, and lawyer who led the ANC's turn to armed struggle, spent twenty-seven years in prison — much of it breaking rocks on Robben Island — and walked out in 1990 without bitterness, to negotiate the end of apartheid with the men who had jailed him. He made reconciliation a strategy rather than a sentiment: learning Afrikaans to understand his jailers, donning the Springbok jersey to disarm white fears, and building a Government of National Unity with his former enemy. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission traded justice for truth to hold a fracturing nation together. Then, in the rarest act of all, he served a single term and stepped down — Washington's move, repeated on another continent.",

  castro: "A lawyer and baseball-loving son of a wealthy planter who turned guerrilla, Fidel Castro led a small band from the Sierra Maestra to topple Batista in 1959 and then ruled Cuba for nearly half a century — outlasting eleven U.S. presidents, a fact he made the core of his myth. He fused a party-state with his own marathon-speech persona, survived the Bay of Pigs and near-annihilation in the Missile Crisis, and projected influence far beyond his island's size while his people bore embargo and shortage. A relentless micromanager, he meddled in everything from cattle breeding to coffee planting, and solved the succession problem the personalist way — handing power to his brother Raúl. Revolutionary icon or tropical tyrant depending on the teller, he was the Cold War's most durable personality.",

  // ---------------- 21st-century leaders ----------------
  gwbush: "A Yale-and-Harvard-educated scion of a political dynasty who found direction late — oilman, Texas Rangers co-owner, born-again governor — and reached the White House in the disputed 2000 election as a 'compassionate conservative.' The September 11 attacks eight months in transformed his presidency into a wartime one; the self-described 'decider' ran an MBA-style operation of clear lanes, gut calls, and prized loyalty, delegating vast authority to heavyweights Cheney and Rumsfeld. The Afghanistan and Iraq wars — the latter launched on flawed intelligence — defined and dogged him, though his second term corrected course with the surge, taken against most advice. Tax cuts, the financial crisis, and PEPFAR's AIDS relief round out a consequential, contested record.",

  putin: "A middling KGB officer in Dresden when the Wall fell, Putin rose through St. Petersburg city hall and Yeltsin's Kremlin to the presidency in 2000, and has ruled Russia — as president or prime minister — ever since. He applied the case officer's method to an entire state: cultivate, compromise, control, building a 'vertical of power' that hollowed formal institutions and vested real authority in a court of security-service veterans, licensed oligarchs, and personal loyalists. He balances rival clans as sole arbiter, restored order and pride after the chaotic 1990s, and turned increasingly toward repression at home and revanchism abroad. The 2022 invasion of Ukraine exposed the strategic blind spot of long-tenure autocracy — a leader insulated from the bad news his own system is built to suppress.",

  obama: "Son of a Kenyan father and Kansan mother, raised in Hawaii and Indonesia, a community organizer and constitutional-law lecturer whose 2008 election as the first Black U.S. president was itself a historic rupture. He governed as a deliberative professor — mapping options in long sessions, comfortable with probabilistic '55-45' calls, and reserving soaring rhetoric for hinge moments. He passed the Affordable Care Act, steered the economy out of the 2008 crash, ordered the raid that killed bin Laden, and struck the Paris climate and Iran nuclear accords, all while facing implacable congressional obstruction and a coarsening politics. Cool, cerebral, and cautious, he centralized foreign policy in a tight NSC and left office as admired as he was polarizing.",

  xi: "A 'princeling' whose revolutionary father was purged under Mao — sending the teenage Xi to labor in a rural cave village — he rose patiently through the provinces to become China's most powerful leader since Mao himself. Since 2012 he has reversed Deng's collective-leadership norms, using a sweeping anti-corruption campaign as both genuine cleanup and factional purge, abolishing presidential term limits, and enshrining 'Xi Jinping Thought' in the constitution. He chairs virtually every important body personally, tightening Party control over economy, society, and the military while projecting power abroad through the Belt and Road and a harder nationalism. His wager is that concentrated authority beats institutionalized succession — reopening precisely the single-point-of-failure question that Deng's reforms had tried to close.",

  merkel: "A quantum chemist raised in communist East Germany, Angela Merkel entered politics only as the Wall fell and rose, underestimated at every step, to lead Germany and effectively Europe for sixteen years. She governed with a scientist's patience — gather data, wait, let opponents exhaust themselves, then move last — a method so distinctive that 'to merkel' became a German verb for cautious indecision-as-strategy. Low-ego and consensus-driven, she mastered coalition management by de-dramatizing every crisis, steering the euro's near-collapse, the 2015 refugee influx, and a pandemic. Her critics' charge — that she managed rather than shaped, deferring the hard structural choices — is precisely the steward's tradeoff, made by the most powerful woman of her era.",

  erdogan: "A working-class Istanbul boy and semi-professional footballer who rose through Islamist politics and a popular, reforming mayoralty — even jailed briefly for reciting a poem — to dominate Turkey for two decades. His early years as prime minister brought genuine liberalization and growth; over time, and especially after surviving the 2016 coup attempt, he consolidated a majoritarian personal rule, remaking the constitution into an executive presidency and bringing courts, media, and party to heel. He treats the ballot box as the sole legitimacy that counts, empowering technocrats and then discarding them (Babacan, Davutoğlu) in favor of loyalty over expertise. Ambitious abroad and polarizing at home, he embodies the 21st-century drift from elected reformer to elected strongman.",

  abe: "Scion of a political dynasty whose grandfather was a prime minister, Shinzo Abe flamed out in a scattered first term in 2006-07, then returned in 2012 to become Japan's longest-serving prime minister by fixing the office itself. He built a Kantei-centered government, taking control of senior bureaucratic appointments so the prime minister's office could finally steer the powerful ministries that had long outlasted their nominal masters. 'Abenomics' — monetary easing, fiscal spending, structural reform — sought to jolt Japan from decades of stagnation, while he pursued a more assertive foreign and defense policy and courted allies personally. Assassinated in 2022, he left a strengthened premiership and an unfinished argument about Japan's economic and strategic direction.",

  modi: "A former tea-seller and lifelong RSS organizer who rose as chief minister of Gujarat — its boom and its 2002 communal riots both on his ledger — to become India's most dominant prime minister in generations. He governs as a centralized communicator, reaching voters directly through radio addresses and social media around party and press alike, with Amit Shah as enforcer-organizer and Hindutva nationalism as the mobilizing frame. Power concentrates in the PMO and a trusted circle of Gujarat-era officials; big-bang decisions like the 2016 demonetization are taken in tiny rooms. Wildly popular and deeply polarizing, he has reshaped Indian politics, the economy, and the secular-national settlement more than anyone since Nehru.",

  trump: "A Queens real-estate heir turned brand, casino operator, and reality-television star who had never held office when he won the presidency in 2016, and returned to win it again in 2024 — the only president to serve non-consecutive terms since Grover Cleveland. He governs by disruption: dominance framing, prized unpredictability as leverage, and direct mass communication that displaces institutional channels, running a hub-and-spoke court of rival aides subordinated to personal loyalty and televised performance. His first term brought tax cuts, deregulation, three Supreme Court justices, and two impeachments; its end — disputed results and January 6 — was itself unprecedented. He delegates execution but never the narrative: every storyline, by design, returns to him.",

  ardern: "A Mormon-raised former DJ and policy staffer who became New Zealand's prime minister at 37 and, weeks later, one of the few world leaders to give birth in office. Her defining register was empathetic crisis communication: after the 2019 Christchurch mosque attacks she donned a headscarf, embraced the Muslim community, and swiftly reformed gun laws; during COVID she rallied a 'team of five million' behind an elimination strategy. She asserted kindness as a governing strategy and drew global admiration, though critics charged that delivery lagged the communicative brilliance. In 2023 she resigned unprompted, saying she had 'nothing left in the tank' — a rare, candid exit at the height of fame.",

  zelensky: "A comedian and actor who played a schoolteacher-turned-president on television, then won the actual Ukrainian presidency in 2019 on an anti-corruption, outsider wave — dismissed by many as a lightweight until war made him a wartime leader. When Russia invaded in February 2022 and offered him evacuation, his reported reply — 'I need ammunition, not a ride' — became the war's defining line and the hinge of his leadership. He turned a performer's toolkit — timing, staging, reading an audience — into a global persuasion campaign, addressing parliaments nightly and binding Western support through the presidential office. Leaving military operations to professional command, he ran the war of narrative and alliance; the friction came when those lanes crossed.",

  lula: "A metalworker who lost a finger on the factory floor, led the strikes that helped end Brazil's dictatorship, and founded the Workers' Party, Lula rose from poverty and only four years of schooling to the presidency he'd sought three times. His first tenure (2003-2010) paired orthodox economics to calm markets with Bolsa Família cash transfers that lifted millions from poverty, leaving office with sky-high approval. Then came the fall — a corruption conviction and 580 days in prison — and an astonishing return, winning the 2022 election to a third term. A consummate negotiator who keeps political brokerage personal while delegating the economy to reassure investors, he embodies coalition presidentialism's promise and its scandals alike.",

  taizong: "Born Li Shimin, the second son of a Sui garrison commander, he was the military engine of his family's rise — winning the campaigns that founded the Tang while his elder brother held the position of heir. In 626 he resolved that arithmetic at the Xuanwu Gate, killing both brothers in an ambush and compelling his father's abdication; the reign that followed spent twenty-three years earning the legitimacy its first act destroyed. He made criticism a standing institution rather than a risk, keeping Wei Zheng — a man who had urged the dead brother to have him killed — as an official whose job was to tell him he was wrong, and reportedly mourned him as a lost mirror. The Zhenguan era became East Asia's enduring template for good government, exported to Korea, Japan and Vietnam, and the record of his court debates outlived his dynasty as a manual for rulers. The shadow over all of it: he pressured the court diarists to let him read and shape the record, so the most admired reign in Chinese history is also one of its most carefully edited.",

  robertbruce: "A Norman-Scottish earl with a claim to the throne and a long record of switching sides, Bruce murdered his rival John Comyn before an altar in 1306, had himself crowned weeks later, and was a hunted fugitive within months — his lands seized, three brothers executed, his wife and daughter imprisoned. What followed was the most complete comeback in medieval Britain: eight years of guerrilla war, castles slighted rather than held, pitched battle refused until Bannockburn in 1314 handed him a kingdom. He governed as he had fought, absorbing former enemies rather than destroying them, and in 1320 the Declaration of Arbroath framed his kingship as a compact the community of the realm could withdraw — a startling document to issue about your own authority. He died of illness in 1329 having won recognition of Scottish independence, leaving a five-year-old heir and a second war of independence to follow.",

  chiang: "A Zhejiang salt merchant's son trained in Japanese military academies, Chiang rose through Sun Yat-sen's movement to command the Whampoa Academy — the officer network that became his real constituency for fifty years. He unified much of China in the Northern Expedition, then turned on his Communist allies in the 1927 Shanghai massacre, spending the next two decades fighting warlords, Japanese invasion and Mao simultaneously. Austere, devout and remote, he governed by balancing rival cliques and distrusting talent, directing divisions personally by telephone while inflation and corruption hollowed out his base; the civil war he lost in 1949 was lost administratively as much as militarily. On Taiwan he imposed four decades of martial law and the White Terror, and also presided over the land reform and industrial policy that built the island's economy — the authoritarian modernizer's ledger, with both columns long.",

  hueylong: "Born to a modest farming family in Louisiana's hill country, Long talked his way through a law degree in under a year and onto the Railroad Commission at 25, then took the governorship in 1928 promising roads, free textbooks and war on Standard Oil — and delivered all three. He also took the legislature, the courts, the police, the tax rolls and every job in the state, building the most complete political machine in American history and surviving impeachment by buying the senators who would have convicted him. As U.S. senator he broke with Roosevelt and launched Share Our Wealth — 'Every Man a King' — a redistribution scheme whose seven million members made him the most plausible threat to FDR's 1936 reelection. Shot in the state capitol he had built and dead at 42, he remains American politics' central case study in how much good and how much damage one charismatic machine-builder can do at once.",
  rjdaley: "The only child of an Irish sheet-metal worker in Bridgeport, Chicago's stockyards neighbourhood, Daley worked in the yards, took a law degree at night from DePaul, and rose through the Democratic organisation one rung at a time — precinct captain, state legislator, state revenue director under Adlai Stevenson, Cook County clerk. In 1953 he became county party chairman and in 1955 mayor, and he held both posts until he died of a heart attack in December 1976. He never left Bridgeport, went to Mass every morning, and ran the city as a machine that also worked: O'Hare became the world's busiest airport, the expressways and McCormick Place were built, the Loop was rebuilt, and Chicago became 'the city that works'. The same machine delivered Illinois — narrowly — for John Kennedy in 1960, built the segregated high-rises of the Robert Taylor Homes along the Dan Ryan Expressway, outmanoeuvred Martin Luther King's 1966 open-housing campaign, ordered police to 'shoot to kill' arsonists after King's assassination, and presided over what an official inquiry called a police riot at the 1968 Democratic convention. His organisation lost City Hall within seven years of his death; his son Richard M. Daley won it back in 1989 and held it for twenty-two years.",

  suharto: "The only child of his father's second marriage, a Javanese village irrigation official, Suharto was raised largely by foster families near Yogyakarta, joined the Dutch colonial army in 1940, served the Japanese-sponsored forces and then the independence army, and by 1965 commanded the Army Strategic Reserve as a major general. When the 30 September Movement killed six generals but did not target him, he took command of the army within a day, blamed the Communist Party, and took power from Sukarno in stages: the 11 March 1966 order known as Supersemar, acting president in 1967, president in 1968. The army-led killings that destroyed the party are usually put at 500,000 to over a million dead — an early army figure was 78,500 and some claims run to three million — and the count remains contested. His New Order rested on an army with posts down to village level, on Golkar, which won every election from 1971 to 1997, and on Western-trained economists who ended hyperinflation and, with oil revenue and foreign aid, delivered growth of about 7 per cent a year and a large fall in poverty. It also rested on censorship, the 1975 invasion and occupation of East Timor, the 1983–85 'mysterious shootings', and the enrichment of his family and friends. The 1997 currency crisis and the riots of May 1998 ended it: he resigned on 21 May 1998. He was never tried — charges over money diverted from his foundations were halted on medical grounds in 2000 and formally dropped in 2006 — and he died on 27 January 2008. What his family took is contested: Transparency International's 2004 estimate of US$15–35 billion is an estimate, not a finding, and the family denied it.",

  parkchunghee: "Born in 1917 to a poor farming family near Gumi in North Gyeongsang, Park taught primary school, then entered the Manchukuo army's military academy and the Imperial Japanese Army Academy, and served as a Manchukuo army officer until 1945 — a record his critics keep at the centre of every argument about him. After 1945 he joined the Korean constabulary; in 1948 he was arrested over alleged membership of a communist cell, sentenced to death and reprieved, and he returned to the army in the Korean War. On 16 May 1961, as a major general, he led the coup that ended the Second Republic; he set up the KCIA, chaired the junta and, after pledging under American pressure to restore civilian rule, won the 1963 presidential election by about 156,000 votes. Five-year plans, export targets, the 1965 treaty with Japan, some 320,000 troops sent to Vietnam, the Saemaul Undong from 1970 and the Heavy and Chemical Industry drive from 1973 took a poor agrarian country into the industrial world. Politically he had the constitution amended for a third term in 1969, narrowly beat Kim Dae-jung in 1971, and in October 1972 dissolved the assembly and imposed the Yushin constitution: unlimited six-year terms chosen by an electoral college, rule by emergency decree, a third of the assembly on his nomination. The KCIA kidnapped Kim Dae-jung from a Tokyo hotel in 1973, and opponents were often held without trial and tortured. After the Pusan–Masan protests of October 1979 his KCIA director, Kim Jae-gyu, shot him on 26 October; whether the killing was planned or spontaneous is still unsettled. Historians divide on how much of the growth was his own design rather than the inheritance of the 1950s, American aid and Japanese money, and on what it cost. His daughter Park Geun-hye was elected president in 2013 and was impeached and removed in 2017.",

  mussolini: "The son of a blacksmith and socialist in Predappio in the Romagna and of a schoolteacher, Mussolini trained as a teacher, spent 1902–04 in Switzerland as a migrant worker and socialist agitator, and in 1912 became editor of the Socialist Party's daily, Avanti!, raising its circulation from about 20,000 to about 100,000. In 1914 he broke with the party by campaigning for Italy to enter the war, founded his own paper, Il Popolo d'Italia, and was expelled; he served at the front and was wounded in 1917. In Milan on 23 March 1919 he founded the Fasci di Combattimento, whose squads spent 1920–22 burning socialist offices and beating opponents, often with the tolerance of landowners, police and prefects. The March on Rome at the end of October 1922 brought an appointment rather than a seizure: the King refused to sign a decree of martial law and made him prime minister of a coalition. The Acerbo law of 1923 and the April 1924 election, won with about 64 per cent amid violence, gave him a majority. The murder of the Socialist deputy Giacomo Matteotti in June 1924 nearly broke him; on 3 January 1925 he answered by claiming responsibility for all that had happened, and over the next two years built the dictatorship — press laws, the dissolution of the other parties, a special tribunal and internal exile (confino). The Lateran Pacts of 11 February 1929 settled the quarrel with the Church. He invaded Ethiopia in October 1935, using poison gas, proclaimed an empire in May 1936, drew close to Hitler, introduced antisemitic racial laws in 1938, and on 10 June 1940 took Italy into the Second World War. Defeats in Greece, North Africa and Russia and the Allied landing in Sicily led the Grand Council of Fascism to vote against him, 19 to 8, in the night of 24–25 July 1943; the King dismissed him that afternoon and had him arrested. Freed by German commandos from the Gran Sasso on 12 September 1943, he headed the Italian Social Republic at Salò under German control, whose court sentenced his son-in-law Galeazzo Ciano to death in January 1944. Caught by partisans at Dongo, he was shot at Giulino di Mezzegra on 28 April 1945, and his body was hung in Milan's Piazzale Loreto the next day. How far Italians consented to his rule — Renzo De Felice's 'years of consensus' — is still disputed among historians.",

  franco: "Born in the naval town of Ferrol in Galicia on 4 December 1892, the son of a naval paymaster who later abandoned the family, Franco entered the Toledo Infantry Academy in 1907 because the navy academy had closed its entry, and made his career in the colonial war in Morocco. Shot in the abdomen at El Biutz in 1916, he recovered, became the youngest major in the army, second-in-command and then commander of the Spanish Legion, led the first wave ashore at Alhucemas in 1925, and was promoted brigadier general in 1926 at 33. He directed the General Military Academy at Zaragoza until the Republic closed it in 1931, commanded the suppression of the Asturias miners' rising in October 1934, and was chief of the general staff in 1935. Posted to the Canary Islands in 1936, he joined the military conspiracy late; on 18–19 July he flew to Morocco to take command of the Army of Africa, which German and Italian aircraft ferried to the mainland. On 1 October 1936 his fellow generals in the Burgos junta made him Generalísimo and head of state, and in April 1937 he fused the Falange and the Carlists into a single party under himself. The war ended in April 1939 with the Nationalist victory. The repression during and after it — mass executions, military courts, camps, a prison population the regime itself put at over 270,000 — killed somewhere between about 100,000 and 200,000 people by the commonest estimates, which remain disputed, and sent about half a million into exile. He met Hitler at Hendaye on 23 October 1940, asked for more than Hitler would give, and stayed out of the war while sending the Blue Division to fight the Soviet Union. Isolated after 1945, he was rescued by the Cold War: the Concordat with the Vatican and the bases agreement with the United States in 1953, UN membership in 1955. The 1947 Law of Succession made Spain a kingdom with him as head of state for life; in 1957 he brought Opus Dei technocrats into government, and their 1959 Stabilisation Plan opened the 'Spanish miracle' of 1959–74. On 22 July 1969 he named Prince Juan Carlos his successor as king. He gave up the premiership to Luis Carrero Blanco in June 1973, saw him killed by ETA that December, and confirmed five death sentences in September 1975 despite appeals from the Pope and much of Europe. He died in Madrid on 20 November 1975 after a long illness; Juan Carlos, the heir he had chosen to preserve the regime, dismantled it within three years.",

  salazar: "Born on 28 April 1889 in Vimieiro, near Santa Comba Dão, the son of a smallholder who managed a local estate, Salazar studied at the Viseu seminary from 1900 to 1908, then read law at Coimbra, where he became a professor of economic policy and finance and an organiser of Catholic political action. Elected a deputy in 1921, he attended the Chamber once. After the military coup of May 1926 he served for a few days as finance minister and resigned; in April 1928, with the military dictatorship near bankruptcy, he took the post again on condition that he could veto the spending of every ministry, and produced the first budget surplus in years. He became President of the Council of Ministers on 5 July 1932 and gave Portugal the corporative constitution of 1933, approved in a plebiscite in which abstentions were counted as votes in favour. His Estado Novo rested on the National Union (a 'non-party'), on censorship, on the political police (PVDE from 1933, PIDE from 1945) and the Tarrafal camp in Cape Verde, and on alliance with the Church, sealed by the 1940 Concordat. He backed Franco in the Spanish Civil War, kept Portugal neutral in the Second World War while selling tungsten to both sides, granted Britain and then the United States bases in the Azores from 1943, and brought Portugal into NATO as a founding member in 1949. The 1958 presidential campaign of General Humberto Delgado frightened the regime into abolishing direct presidential elections; PIDE agents murdered Delgado in 1965. From 1961 he fought colonial wars in Angola, Guinea and Mozambique rather than decolonise, and lost Goa to India that December; he never visited the African territories he defended. On 3 August 1968 he fell at his summer residence in the fort of Santo António da Barra at Estoril; a brain haemorrhage followed, and in late September President Américo Tomás replaced him with Marcelo Caetano. Never told that he had been replaced, he received ministers and visitors at São Bento as if still in office until his death on 27 July 1970. The regime outlived him by less than four years, ending in the army's Carnation Revolution of 25 April 1974. Whether it was fascist is still argued: most historians call it conservative authoritarian, with fascist trappings it later shed.",

  pinochet: "Born in Valparaíso on 25 November 1915, the son of a customs official, Pinochet entered the Military School in the early 1930s and spent four decades as a staff and garrison officer with a reputation for obedience: instructor at the War Academy, a mission to Ecuador's war college from 1956, author of a textbook on geopolitics (1968), division general in 1971 and chief of the army staff in 1972. On 23 August 1973 Salvador Allende made him army commander-in-chief on the recommendation of the outgoing Carlos Prats, who thought him a constitutionalist. On 9 September he became the last of the service chiefs to sign on to the coup the navy and air force had already planned; on 11 September the air force bombed La Moneda and Allende killed himself inside. The junta of the four commanders — army, navy, air force and Carabineros — closed Congress, banned the left's parties and in the following months killed, tortured or drove into exile tens of thousands; the 'Caravan of Death' that October killed at least seventy prisoners in provincial garrisons. Decree Law 527 of June 1974 made him Supreme Chief of the Nation, and that December he took the title of President. DINA, the secret police run by Manuel Contreras and answerable to him, murdered Prats in Buenos Aires in 1974 and the former minister Orlando Letelier in Washington in 1976. From 1975 a team of Chicago-trained economists imposed shock stabilisation, privatisation, open trade and, in 1980–81, privatised pensions. A 1978 decree law amnestied the crimes committed since the coup, the air force commander Gustavo Leigh was forced out that July, and a constitution approved in a plebiscite on 11 September 1980 — 67 per cent in favour, held without electoral registers — gave him an eight-year term and set a plebiscite on his continuation for 1988. The banking crash of 1982 and the mass protests that began in 1983 shook the regime without breaking it, and he survived an ambush by the Manuel Rodríguez Patriotic Front in September 1986. On 5 October 1988 he lost the plebiscite, 56 to 44 per cent; the other commanders would not back a reversal, and on 11 March 1990 he handed the presidency to Patricio Aylwin, keeping the army command until March 1998 and then taking a seat in the Senate for life. Arrested in London on 16 October 1998 on a Spanish warrant, he was held for some sixteen months and released on medical grounds in March 2000. Back in Chile he was stripped of immunity, indicted for human-rights crimes and — after a U.S. Senate inquiry in 2004 exposed secret accounts at Riggs Bank and elsewhere — for tax fraud; he died on 10 December 2006 with cases still pending, never tried. Chile's official commissions have recognised about 40,000 victims of the dictatorship, among them 3,065 people killed or disappeared. His economic model, and his constitution, much amended, outlived him.",

  peron: "Born in Lobos, in the province of Buenos Aires, on 8 October 1895, and raised partly on his father's sheep farms in Patagonia, Perón entered the Military College in 1911, became the army's fencing champion and a teacher of military history at the War College — his Apuntes de historia militar appeared in 1932 — and took part in the coup of 1930 as a junior staff officer. As military attaché in Chile (1936–38) and on a mission to Mussolini's Italy (1939–41) he watched mass politics at close range. A leading member of the GOU, the officers' lodge behind the coup of 4 June 1943, he turned a minor post into a base: head of the National Labour Department from October 1943 and then of a new Secretariat of Labour and Social Welfare, where he settled strikes in the unions' favour, extended collective bargaining, pensions and paid holidays, and promoted the Estatuto del Peón for farm workers in 1944; by mid-1944 he was also war minister and vice-president. He met the actress Eva Duarte at a benefit for the victims of the San Juan earthquake in January 1944. Forced out by rival officers on 9 October 1945 and held on the island of Martín García, he was freed after workers marched on the Plaza de Mayo on 17 October, and spoke from the balcony of the Casa Rosada that night; he married Eva days later. Elected president on 24 February 1946 against a coalition backed by the U.S. ambassador Spruille Braden ('Braden or Perón'), he nationalised the central bank, the British-owned railways and the telephones, created the IAPI export monopoly and two five-year plans, gave women the vote in 1947 and rewrote the constitution in 1949 to allow his re-election — while impeaching the Supreme Court, buying or closing hostile newspapers (La Prensa was expropriated in 1951) and jailing opponents. Re-elected in November 1951 with 63.5 per cent, he lost Eva to cancer on 26 July 1952, met the end of the post-war boom with austerity, and in 1954–55 picked a fight with the Church. On 16 June 1955 navy aircraft bombed the Plaza de Mayo, killing more than 300 people; in September a military revolt, the self-styled Revolución Libertadora, brought him down, and he left on a Paraguayan gunboat. For seventeen years he directed his banned movement from Paraguay, Panama, Venezuela, the Dominican Republic and, from 1960, Madrid, where he married Isabel Martínez in 1961. Allowed back after Héctor Cámpora won the presidency for the movement in March 1973, he landed on 20 June amid the Ezeiza massacre, in which the Peronist right fired on the Peronist left; Cámpora resigned, and on 23 September Perón was elected with about 62 per cent, Isabel as vice-president. He spent nine months in office trying to discipline the movement's guerrilla left — turning on the Montoneros from the balcony on 1 May 1974 — while his secretary José López Rega built the death squads of the Triple A. He died on 1 July 1974; Isabel's government collapsed into the coup of March 1976. The movement he founded has won most of the Argentine presidential elections held without proscription since.",

  goh: "A ship-broker's executive recruited into politics by the founding generation, Goh spent fourteen years as Singapore's second prime minister doing the hardest job in this index: succeeding Lee Kuan Yew while Lee sat in his cabinet as Senior Minister. He chose to differentiate rather than imitate, promising a 'kinder, gentler' Singapore, opening consultative exercises and loosening the paternalism a few degrees without touching the machinery that produced the results. He steered the economy through the Asian financial crisis, SARS and the post-9/11 shock, and pushed the integrated-resort and biomedical bets that reshaped the economy after him. Then he did the thing founders' successors usually fail at — groomed Lee Hsien Loong, handed over in 2004, and stayed on in a diminished advisory role rather than a rival one, proving the succession machinery Lee had built actually worked twice.",

  berlusconi: "The son of a Milan bank employee, Berlusconi graduated in law in 1961 with a thesis on the legal aspects of advertising, sang on cruise ships, and made his first fortune in construction: Milano 2, a planned town of about 4,000 flats outside Milan, built by Edilnord between 1970 and 1979. Its cable channel grew into TeleMilano and then Canale 5 (1980); he bought Italia 1 in 1982 and Rete 4 in 1984, and when magistrates ordered his stations' national broadcasts switched off in October 1984, Bettino Craxi's government restored them by decree — an arrangement the 1990 Mammì law made permanent. By then Fininvest held the three main private national channels, the advertising house Publitalia, AC Milan (from 1986) and, after a long legal battle, Mondadori. When the Mani Pulite investigations destroyed the parties that had governed since the war, he announced in a videotaped message to the evening news on 26 January 1994 that he was 'taking the field', launched Forza Italia, and won the March 1994 election with two separate alliances — the Northern League in the north and the post-fascist National Alliance in the centre and south. That government fell when the League withdrew in December 1994. He lost to Romano Prodi in 1996, won in 2001 after signing a 'Contract with the Italians' on television, and governed until 2006 — his second cabinet was the longest-lasting of the Republic — and returned in 2008 at the head of the People of Freedom. He resigned on 12 November 2011, as Italy's borrowing costs soared in the euro crisis and his majority dissolved; Mario Monti succeeded him. The conflict of interest — a prime minister who owned the main private broadcaster and influenced appointments at the public one — was never resolved by law; in April 2002, in Sofia, he publicly attacked two RAI journalists and a comedian, whose programmes were then dropped. He was prosecuted many times. Most cases ended in acquittal or expired under the statute of limitations, some after his majorities had shortened limitation periods or reduced the offence of false accounting; the 2013 conviction in the Ruby case was overturned on appeal in 2014. One case produced a definitive conviction: tax fraud in Mediaset's purchase of film rights, a four-year sentence (three years cancelled by a 2006 pardon) upheld by the Court of Cassation on 1 August 2013. He served it as community service at a care home in Cesano Boscone from May 2014 to March 2015, and was expelled from the Senate on 27 November 2013 under the Severino law. His co-founder Marcello Dell'Utri was definitively convicted in 2014 of external association with the Mafia; Berlusconi was not a defendant in that case and always denied any Mafia link. He returned to the European Parliament in 2019 and the Senate in 2022, and died in Milan on 12 June 2023.",

  kissinger: "A Jewish refugee from Fürth who fled Nazi Germany at fifteen, returned as a U.S. Army interpreter, and rose through Harvard to become the most famous — and most argued-over — statesman of the American century. As Nixon's national security adviser and then secretary of state, uniquely holding both posts at once, he ran the opening to China, détente with Moscow, shuttle diplomacy after the Yom Kippur War, and the Paris talks that won him a Nobel Peace Prize the North Vietnamese co-laureate declined. His method was the back-channel: secrecy so complete that cabinet colleagues and often the State Department learned of policy after the fact, concentrating the apparatus around one man's judgment. The same record carries Cambodia, the tilt toward Pakistan in 1971, and Chile — which is why he remains the sharpest available test of whether realism in foreign policy is statecraft or alibi.",

  biden: "A working-class kid from Scranton who lost his first wife and infant daughter in a car crash weeks after his 1972 Senate win, Biden spent half a century in Washington accumulating relationships — thirty-six years in the Senate, eight as Obama's vice president — before winning the presidency on his third try in 2020. He governed on Senate-style relationship politics scaled up, spending decades of personal capital to assemble legislative and alliance coalitions: major infrastructure, semiconductor, and climate bills, and a rebuilt Western front behind Ukraine. He empowered a seasoned cabinet and disciplined NSC staffed by longtime aides. In the end, the question that had shadowed his career — his age — became the delegation question, and he stepped aside from the 2024 race."
};

